# Robinhood Chain mainnet (Arbitrum Orbit L2) — chainId 4663
RPC_URL=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com

# Second node. Used for the preflight's CROSS-CHECK: the keeper rebuilds the root
# from this node, with an empty cache, and refuses to publish if the two do not
# agree. It is the only check that catches a DATA error rather than a consistency
# one — a node that truncates a page of logs makes holders disappear without
# anything looking wrong in the totals.
#
# Leaving it empty skips the check cleanly. Setting it doubles the time of each
# publication cycle: the second pass replays everything without a cache.
#
# IT MUST SERVE THE FULL LOG HISTORY, back to the token's launch block, for as
# long as the deployment lives. publicnode was used here and failed exactly that
# way on 2026-09-06: it validated a root at 20:32 and could no longer reproduce
# it at 21:20 — 72 entries instead of 82, the oldest holders silently gone. Its
# history window had rolled past the token's genesis after roughly a day, and
# every publication was blocked from then on.
#
# Verify a candidate before trusting it: rebuild an already-published epoch and
# require the claimRoot the chain carries.
#
#   EPOCH_DIR=$(mktemp -d) RPC_URL=<candidate> \
#     npx tsx offchain/src/recompute.ts <distributor> <vault> <upToEpoch>
#
# Measured 2026-09-07: rpc-robinhood.blockmachine.io is a real archive node and
# refuses loudly (a 10,000-block cap, which scanLogs now narrows around), but its
# free endpoint returns 429 on ~2 of 3 concurrent requests — good enough for a
# one-off dispute.ts run, not for a keeper. A managed archive endpoint is the
# right answer here; the key never goes anywhere near front/.
RPC_URL_FALLBACK=https://robinhood-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<KEY>

# Explorers (EIP-3091)
EXPLORER_URL=https://robinhoodchain.blockscout.com
ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=

# Keeper wallet: gas only, holds nothing else. NEVER commit a real key.
KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY=

# IPFS — THE ONLY STATE THAT CAN ACTUALLY BE LOST.
#
# Holders build their proofs from these artifacts. If IPFS_API_URL points at a
# LOCAL kubo, they die with the machine. A pinning service makes the keeper
# entirely disposable: destroy it, restart it elsewhere, nothing is lost.
#
# It must speak the KUBO RPC API — the client posts to /api/v0/add with a Bearer
# token. That rules out services offering only the IPFS Pinning Service spec or
# their own upload endpoint, which is most of them. Filebase exposes a
# kubo-compatible subset and works:
#
#     IPFS_API_URL=https://rpc.filebase.io
#     IPFS_API_KEY=<per-bucket IPFS RPC token, from the console>
IPFS_API_URL=            # http://127.0.0.1:5001 for a local kubo
IPFS_API_KEY=

# READ-BACK gateways. The keeper publishes, reads back through a gateway, and
# only commits the root once it has verified the content really comes back. A
# root without its content is an epoch nobody can claim.
#
# ORDER MATTERS, and the last one is not decoration. The public gateways come
# first because a third party fetching the content is the stronger proof — the
# pinning service serving back what it just pinned proves much less. But they
# rate-limit hard (429 after a busy session, and a 429 is indistinguishable
# from absence), and on launch night that deferred the first root indefinitely
# while the artifact was in fact perfectly retrievable. Filebase's own gateway
# closes that gap: preferred proof first, provider's gateway as the fallback
# that keeps the cycle moving.
IPFS_GATEWAYS=https://ipfs.io/ipfs/,https://dweb.link/ipfs/,https://ipfs.filebase.io/ipfs/

# Our contracts, filled in after deployment (script/Deploy.s.sol)
DISTRIBUTOR=
FEE_VAULT=

# Deployment only (script/Deploy.s.sol)
SAFE_MULTISIG=
DEV_ADDRESS=
# The keeper's public address. It is the ONLY account allowed to publish a root.
# The timelock can rotate it in 48 h if the key leaks.
KEEPER_ADDRESS=

# **The second authority on the three doors that move value to an address
# somebody names** — `Payd.setFactory`, `Treasury.bindPlatform` and
# `Treasury.migrateTreasury`. It approves; it never triggers: the timelock still
# has to schedule and wait out its 48 h, and this key alone can do nothing at
# all.
#
# It exists because no on-chain check can tell a real factory, or a real
# successor treasury, from a contract written for the occasion — everything one
# would read on the destination, the destination writes. So this does not make
# anything "legitimate". It turns one compromised key into two independent ones.
#
# `migrate` itself no longer needs it: its destination is checked with
# `Payd.isVault`, written by `_create` and by nothing else. The successor chain
# that used to guard it was deleted rather than guarded (`FLOWS.md` §7).
#
# Which is worth exactly nothing if it is not held apart. A hardware wallet, in
# different custody from the Safe signers, that signs nothing else and holds
# nothing. `DeployPayd` refuses a deployment where this equals SAFE_MULTISIG —
# the same key twice is code without security.
GENERATION_KEY=

# How many epochs one published root covers. 1 = a root every epoch, the cadence
# the system was designed with and the one the dashboard feels — leave it there
# unless gas becomes the problem.
#
# It is the lever for that. publishRoot is 242,520 gas of the 609,815 an epoch
# costs, and it is the only one of the four that need not run every time: roots
# are cumulative, so one can settle four epochs. At 0.4182 gwei, ETH at $2,455:
#
#     1  (30 min)  $30.05/day       4  (2 h)  $21.09/day       12 (6 h)  $19.10/day
#
# It bottoms out at $18.10/day — fund, anchorEpoch and revealSeed run every epoch
# whatever happens. That floor is the price of a 30-minute epoch and only the
# epoch length, immutable after deployment, can move it.
#
# Raising this does NOT slow the visible accumulation: per-epoch history and
# per-stock totals are read from the chain, and the airdrop follows
# PUSH_INTERVAL_HOURS. It delays how soon a holder can claim BY HAND.
ROOT_INTERVAL_EPOCHS=1

# Airdrop cadence, in hours. The push floor bounds gas by the VALUE distributed;
# this cadence bounds it by the NUMBER OF HOLDERS. Together they divide the bill
# by ~6.5. A holder in a hurry does not wait: claim stays permanently open, they
# just pay their own gas.
PUSH_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# How many published artifacts stay pinned. One per epoch, so 100 is about two
# days of history at 30-minute epochs.
#
# Pinning without unpinning grows without bound, and it grows with the holder
# count: every artifact carries the FULL cumulative table. At 1,000 holders that
# is ~75 MB a day, so a 5 GB plan is gone in two months; at 5,000 holders, in
# thirteen days. Storage would run out exactly when the project succeeds. A
# window keeps the footprint constant — ~157 MB at 1,000 holders, whatever the
# horizon.
#
# Safe because roots are cumulative: claim verifies against the ACTIVE root, the
# front only fetches that one, and dispute.ts recomputes from the chain rather
# than from stored artifacts. Floored at 8 in code.
IPFS_KEEP=100

# Epoch cache. Under Docker this is a volume; deleting it loses nothing, it only
# makes the first tick longer.
EPOCH_DIR=data

# --- Launch parameters (script/Rehearsal.s.sol, used for the real launch too)
# The socials live ONLY in the launch calldata — not in the stored record, not
# in the event — so an empty slot is a link no indexer can ever show. The Pons
# form only ever collected `twitter`; going through the forwarder ourselves is
# what lets us fill the rest.
# The launch's NAME. The ticker stays PAYD, so a launchpad search on the symbol
# still returns both this launch and the earlier one -- a deliberate, recorded
# trade-off, not an oversight. See DECISIONS.md.
TOKEN_NAME=Payd Protocol
TOKEN_SYMBOL=PAYD
CREATOR_TAX_BPS=400
TOKEN_LOGO=ipfs://<the pinned logo>
TOKEN_DESCRIPTION=Trading fees become real stock, paid to holders every 30 minutes.
TOKEN_X=https://x.com/PaydRH
TOKEN_WEBSITE=https://paydprotocol.eth.limo
# Telegram and Discord go out EMPTY, on purpose. The slots have no setter, so a
# chat linked here is linked for the life of the token -- and an abandoned or
# squatted group under our own name is a scam channel we hand our holders and
# can never unlink. X and the site are enough to launch with; a chat can be
# announced from either of them later, where it can also be withdrawn.
TOKEN_TELEGRAM=
TOKEN_DISCORD=
TOKEN_FARCASTER=

# The first buy, inside the launch transaction — the one buy nobody can get in
# front of. MIN_TOKENS_OUT bounds it: 0.01 ETH bought 5,622,965 tokens on a fork
# of the current config, so a floor around 5,000,000e18 is honest slack.
BUY_AMOUNT_WEI=10000000000000000
MIN_TOKENS_OUT=5000000000000000000000000
# Who receives them. Defaults to the vault's DEPLOYER, i.e. the Safe.
# BUY_RECIPIENT=

# --- Telegram announcer (offchain/src/telegram.ts). Holds no key, sends no
# transaction: it reads logs and posts. Leave TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN empty to have
# it print the messages instead of sending them.
#
# CADENCE, before you point it at a channel: at 30-minute epochs that is ~48
# "bought" plus ~48 "LIVE" messages a day. Ninety-six notifications is how a
# channel gets muted. TELEGRAM_EPOCH_EVERY posts one buy in N, and
# TELEGRAM_ROOT_EVERY one claim announcement in N; the airdrop is already one
# message per wave whatever happens.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# Discord is a WEBHOOK, not a bot application: this only ever talks one way, so
# there is no gateway to hold open and no token to rotate. Channel Settings ->
# Integrations -> Webhooks. Treat the URL as a secret: whoever holds it can post
# as the channel.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Shared by both announcers. Each keeps its own cursor, so they can run
# together, separately, or one after the other without repeating themselves.
ANNOUNCE_POLL_SECONDS=30
ANNOUNCE_EPOCH_EVERY=1
ANNOUNCE_ROOT_EVERY=1
# ANNOUNCE_FROM_BLOCK=   # backfill on purpose; default is "start at the head"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the second key
#
# `Distributor.coSigner`, when named, makes `publishRoot` require a signature by
# a second key over the exact root being published. It is not a second secret:
# `offchain/src/cosign.ts` replays the epochs from its OWN node and signs only
# what it reproduces, so a keeper key alone cannot publish a false root.
#
# RUN IT ON A DIFFERENT HOST, AGAINST A DIFFERENT RPC. Co-locating the signer
# with the keeper rebuilds one key with extra steps: what the second key buys is
# a second COMPUTATION, and that only exists if the machine is another one.
#
#   on the co-signer host:  COSIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x… RPC_URL=<its own node> \
#                           DISTRIBUTOR=0x… pnpm --filter offchain cosign
#   on the keeper host:     COSIGNER_URL=http://<that host>:8787
#
# The co-signer's wallet needs gas for `heartbeat()` and nothing else — it never
# publishes. If it stops heartbeating for CO_SIGNER_GRACE (3 h, six epochs) the
# pinned keeper publishes alone again rather than the vault stopping, and both
# `keeper` and `watch` say so loudly, every round.
#
# COSIGNER_FROM_BLOCK is where the request policing starts. It backfills from 0
# by default, so a co-signer switched on today still sees what a keeper put on
# the record under the PREVIOUS one — starting at the head was how that went
# unseen (docs/AUDIT_FIXES_2.md §3). Set it once the backfill has been done, to
# whatever block the last round reached.
#
# COSIGNER_ADDRESS is read by script/DeployPayd.s.sol and travels in
# `Payd.Wiring`. **Set it before the deploy or the platform's own vault is
# single-key for 48 h**: Payd mints that vault in its constructor, and
# `setCoSigner` is onlyTimelock behind MIN_DELAY and reaches no vault already
# minted. The deploy prints it and shouts if it is zero.
COSIGNER_ADDRESS=
COSIGNER_URL=
COSIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY=
COSIGNER_PORT=8787
COSIGNER_FROM_BLOCK=

# ------------------------------------------------------- the custody checks
#
# `pnpm --filter offchain custody funding|attest|verify` is docs/LAUNCH_2026_12_09.md
# §1.2 and §1.3, which were prose until 2026-09-12.
#
# `funding` needs DISTRIBUTOR and an RPC_URL pointed at the ARCHIVE endpoint —
# it bisects eth_getBalance through history, and a pruning node makes it
# confidently wrong rather than unavailable.
# `attest` needs KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY on one host and COSIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY on the
# other; it signs hostname + a HASH of the RPC (never the URL: it holds a key).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the watcher
#
# `offchain/src/watch.ts` replays every published root and shouts on a
# divergence. It holds no key and sends no transaction, so run it on a third
# machine — it is what covers the two states where the co-signature is NOT in
# force: a vault whose co-signer was never named, and one whose co-signer has
# lapsed past the grace.
#
#   DISTRIBUTOR=0x… FEE_VAULT=0x… pnpm --filter offchain watch
#
# A Discord or Slack webhook. Without it, the console is the alert.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The timelock, for `watch`. Everything the Safe can ever do is announced by
# `CallScheduled` one full 48-hour delay before it happens -- including
# `grantRole`, `revokeRole` and `updateDelay`, which the Safe reaches THROUGH the
# timelock because TimelockController grants DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE to itself. A Safe
# acting against the protocol can therefore add its own proposer, remove the
# Safe, and set the delay to zero, at one delay each.
#
# That announcement is the whole of the defence. Without this variable nothing
# reads it, and `watch` says so once per round rather than passing quietly.
TIMELOCK=
